1
00:00:01,260 --> 00:00:06,540
Today, we're celebrating a major milestone in the life of the International Space Station.

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:12,140
25 years ago, on March 19, 2001, the team at the Payload Operations Integration Center

3
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:17,760
at the Marshall Space Flight Center in Huntsville, Alabama began 24-7 payload operations.

4
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:21,900
This was the beginning of a partnership between the ground and the crew that has enabled

5
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:27,480
more than 4,000 different scientific investigations over the course of 25 years.

6
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:33,940
Those investigations have resulted in over 4,400 papers published in scientific journals.

7
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:39,200
It has taken incredible coordination, both on Earth and in orbit, to make this possible,

8
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:44,840
and now is the perfect time to highlight some key milestones along the way.

9
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:50,760
More than 27,000 shifts have been operated by over 400 certified flight controllers to

10
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:54,060
keep operations going continuously.

11
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:59,120
Hundreds of ops experts have authored thousands of procedures and planning products for an expansive

12
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:05,100
community of payload developers and scientists around the world.

13
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:10,740
Payload facilities on orbit have been powered on for a collective total of over 2.6 million

14
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:16,180
hours, providing essential resources for a wide variety of experiments.

15
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:22,480
In 2019, during Expedition 61, a time I personally remember very well, we set the

16
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:32,420
record for most science hours performed by the crew in a single week at 153 hours.

17
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:41,100
The crew has performed a total of over 43,000 hours of NASA, ESA, JAXA and CSA science arms.

18
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:47,340
The Alpha Magnetic Spectrometer has collected over 260 billion cosmic rays in the search

19
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:48,980
for dark matter.

20
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:54,920
Cold Atom Lab achieved temperatures colder than space, reaching hundreds of nanokalbin,

21
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:59,760
mere fractions of a degree above Absolent Zero.

22
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:04,160
Over a million students have participated in ham radio contacts, inspiring the next

23
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:07,720
generation of scientists and explorers.

24
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:11,920
Astronauts have grown more than 50 species of plants on station, including tomatoes,

25
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:15,000
bok choy, romaine lettuce, and chili peppers.

26
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Delicious.

27
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,500
I'll never forget how fresh that mizuna lettuce tasted and how excited I was to have a fresh

28
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:26,420
space salad for the first time during Expedition 61.

29
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:31,700
The Space Acceleration Measurement System, SAMS-2, has been operating since Increment

30
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:36,900
2 and has collected nearly 1 million hours of acceleration data.

31
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:43,340
MSG has supported 72 different payloads with nearly 100,000 operational hours and

32
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,560
it was built in spec for only 10,000.

33
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:52,000
The NASA Road and Research Program, another one close to my heart, conducted 21 successful

34
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,960
science missions over a period of 11 years.

35
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:00,240
Scientific objectives included efforts to improve health outcomes on Earth and inform basic

36
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:05,480
physiological changes which may be applied to astronaut health during future exploration

37
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:06,980
missions.

38
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:12,280
All of this dedication and hard work has led to breakthroughs in fields as diverse

39
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:19,940
as 3D bioprinting, laser communications, pharmaceuticals, fiber optics, and crew health, which all

40
00:03:19,940 --> 00:03:24,660
foster the commercial economy in low Earth's orbit.

41
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:28,580
We want to send our deepest thanks to the team at Huntsville, a spot we've passed overhead

42
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:34,500
over 40,000 times now for their incredible partnership in operating this orbit in laboratory.

43
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:38,500
We're looking forward to many more milestones as we prepare for missions to the moon

44
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:40,500
and then onward to Mars.

45
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:46,980
Fishing out the boundaries of human exploration and our understanding of the universe.

46
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:48,980
Thank you Huntsville!

